static const char text_keys [] =
	"id: keys\n"
	"title: Key bindings\n"
	"summary: The actions of the editor, their default keys and how to chan"
	"ge them.\n"
	"keywords: keys, bindings, map, unmap, shortcut, rebind, keyboard, sett"
	"ings, mlcfg\n"
	"see: editor, commands\n"
	"\n"
	"Every control key of the editor is bound to a named action. The table "
	"below\n"
	"lists the actions with the keys they have when nothing is changed;\n"
	"`:keys` shows the table as it is now.\n"
	"\n"
	"```keys\n"
	"undo         Ctrl+Z                         undo the last change\n"
	"redo         Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+Shift+Z, Ctrl+R   redo what was undone\n"
	"block-new    Ctrl+Enter, Shift+Enter        start a block, last domain\n"
	"block-pick   Ctrl+B                         start a block, pick the do"
	"main\n"
	"note-toggle  Ctrl+N                         make the line a note, or b"
	"ack\n"
	"domain-next  Alt+Right                      the next domain of the lin"
	"e\n"
	"domain-prev  Alt+Left                       the previous domain of the"
	" line\n"
	"lines-down   Alt+Down                       move the selected lines do"
	"wn\n"
	"lines-up     Alt+Up                         move the selected lines up\n"
	"fold         Alt+Enter                      fold or unfold the block\n"
	"block-next   Ctrl+Down                      go to the next block\n"
	"block-prev   Ctrl+Up                        go to the previous block\n"
	"goto-first   Ctrl+Home                      go to the first line\n"
	"goto-last    Ctrl+End                       go to the last line\n"
	"select-line  Ctrl+L                         select the line\n"
	"select-all   Ctrl+A                         select everything\n"
	"copy         Ctrl+C                         copy the selection or the "
	"line\n"
	"cut          Ctrl+X                         cut the selection or the l"
	"ine\n"
	"paste        Ctrl+V                         paste\n"
	"command      Ctrl+P                         open the command line\n"
	"save         Ctrl+S                         save the session\n"
	"quit         Ctrl+Q                         save and quit\n"
	"steps        F3                             show or hide the line's st"
	"eps\n"
	"help         F1                             open the help\n"
	"watch        F4                             show or hide the watch win"
	"dow\n"
	"plot         F7                             show or hide the line's pl"
	"ot\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Changing a key\n"
	"`:map KEY ACTION` binds a key, `:unmap KEY` removes the binding of a k"
	"ey.\n"
	"Both are written to the `[keys]` section of the settings file, one lin"
	"e per\n"
	"key, and are read again at the next start:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"[keys]\n"
	"Ctrl+K = command\n"
	"Ctrl+Z = none\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"`none` removes a key, so the example gives Ctrl+K the command line and\n"
	"leaves Ctrl+Z without an action. A key bound twice keeps the later lin"
	"e.\n"
	"A line that is not a key or not an action is ignored, and the first on"
	"e is\n"
	"shown in the status line when the program starts.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Writing a key\n"
	"Modifiers joined with `+`, then the key: `Ctrl+Shift+Z`, `Alt+Left`,\n"
	"`F3`, `Ctrl+Enter`. Letters are not case sensitive. The names of the o"
	"ther\n"
	"keys are Enter, Esc, Tab, Backspace, Delete, Insert, Left, Right, Up, "
	"Down,\n"
	"Home, End, PageUp, PageDown and F1 to F12.\n"
	"\n"
	"Only keys that cannot be typed as text can be bound: a key needs Ctrl "
	"or\n"
	"Alt, or is a function key, or is Shift+Enter. Plain characters, the\n"
	"arrows, Enter, Esc, Backspace and Shift with a movement key stay as th"
	"ey\n"
	"are.\n";

static const char text_lambda [] =
	"id: lambda\n"
	"title: Lambda calculus\n"
	"summary: Terms, abstraction, application and step by step reduction.\n"
	"keywords: lambda, calculus, beta, reduction, church, numeral, normal f"
	"orm, trace\n"
	"see: boolean, syntax\n"
	"\n"
	"A line in a block of the lambda domain is a term. The result is its no"
	"rmal\n"
	"form, found by normal order reduction.\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"x            a name\n"
	"\316\273x. t        an abstraction (also \\x. t); \316\273x y. t is "
	"\316\273x. \316\273y. t\n"
	"t u          an application: f x y is (f x) y\n"
	"3            the Church numeral \316\273f x. f (f (f x))\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"The body of a \316\273 goes as far to the right as it can, and an appl"
	"ication\n"
	"binds tighter than a \316\273. The result is written with as few paren"
	"theses as\n"
	"possible, and a binder gets a prime (`y'`) when its name would capture"
	" a\n"
	"free one:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"(\316\273x y. x) y\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"gives `\316\273y'. y`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Definitions\n"
	"`name = term` defines a name for the lines below. A \316\273 that bind"
	"s the same\n"
	"name hides the definition. A definition is kept as the term, not as it"
	"s\n"
	"normal form, so a term without one can be named:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"succ = \316\273n f x. f (n f x)\n"
	"plus = \316\273m n f x. m f (n f x)\n"
	"plus 2 3\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"The last line gives `\316\273f x. f (f (f (f (f x))))`, the Church num"
	"eral 5.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Steps\n"
	"`trace` before a term shows every beta step under the line, up to 60.\n"
	"You do not need to write it: `F3` on a line of the lambda domain shows"
	" the\n"
	"steps of its reduction, and `matlog steps --domain lambda TERM` prints\n"
	"them.\n"
	"\n"
	"# While you type\n"
	"The normal form appears dimmed after the term as soon as it is complet"
	"e.\n"
	"The `\316\273` and the names it binds have the colour of the class `bi"
	"nder`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# When there is no normal form\n"
	"After 5000 steps the line is the error `lambda_no_normal_form`, and wh"
	"en\n"
	"the term grows past two million nodes `lambda_too_big`. A definition i"
	"s\n"
	"still made.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Errors\n"
	"`lambda_unexpected_character`, `lambda_expected_term`,\n"
	"`lambda_expected_parameter`, `lambda_unbalanced_parenthesis`,\n"
	"`lambda_number_too_big`, `lambda_no_normal_form`, `lambda_too_big` and\n"
	"`lambda_bad_definition`.\n";

static const char text_matrices [] =
	"id: matrices\n"
	"title: Matrices and vectors\n"
	"summary: Matrix literals, products, determinants and inverses.\n"
	"keywords: matrix, vector, determinant, inverse, transpose, rank, rref,"
	" dot, cross\n"
	"see: syntax\n"
	"\n"
	"A matrix is a list of rows: `[[1,2],[3,4]]`. A list of numbers\n"
	"`[1,2,3]` is a column vector. The usual operators work on them: `A*B`,\n"
	"`A^T` for the transpose, `A^-1` for the inverse.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Functions\n"
	"`det inv transpose trace rank rref solve identity dot cross norm`.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"[[1,2],[3,4]] * [[0,1],[1,0]]\n"
	"=> [[2, 1], [4, 3]]\n"
	"det([[1,2],[3,4]])\n"
	"=> -2\n"
	"inv([[1,2],[3,4]])\n"
	"=> [[-2, 1], [3/2, -1/2]]\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"Matrices are shown typeset under their line in the editor, and as real\n"
	"matrices in the [export](export).\n";

static const char text_plots [] =
	"id: plots\n"
	"title: Plots\n"
	"summary: Histograms, scatter plots and line plots drawn in the side pa"
	"nel.\n"
	"keywords: plot, scatter, hist, histogram, chart, graph, braille, visua"
	"lization, F7\n"
	"see: statistics, editor, commands, ex-stats-plot-your-data\n"
	"\n"
	"A plot is a value like a number or a matrix. The line shows only a one"
	" line\n"
	"summary, so the editor stays clean; the picture is drawn in the panel "
	"at the\n"
	"right of the editor when you switch it on.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Making a plot\n"
	"```code\n"
	"hist (v, k)       a histogram of the list v in k bins\n"
	"scatter (x, y)    the points (x [i], y [i])\n"
	"plot (x, y)       the same points, each joined to the next by a line\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"hist ([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3], 3)\n"
	"=> histogram of 6 values in 3 bins\n"
	"scatter ([1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6])\n"
	"=> scatter plot of 3 points\n"
	"plot ([1, 2], [5, 7])\n"
	"=> line plot of 2 points\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"The lists are vectors of the same length, as for the statistics functi"
	"ons\n"
	"(see [statistics](statistics)). A plot can be given a name,\n"
	"`bars = hist (data, 5)`, and shown again later by writing the name.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Showing it\n"
	"`F7` on the line of a plot draws it in the panel, `F7` again hides it."
	" The\n"
	"command is `:plot` (`:plot on`, `:plot off`). Any number of plots can "
	"be on;\n"
	"they are drawn one above the other, with a star on the header of the p"
	"lot of\n"
	"the line of the cursor, and what does not fit ends in \"+N more\". Whe"
	"n the\n"
	"watch window is on too, the plots take the upper two thirds of the pan"
	"el.\n"
	"Which plots are on is kept with the session in the settings file, like"
	" the\n"
	"folds, and not in the `.mlog` file. A terminal under 70 columns has no"
	" room\n"
	"for the panel.\n"
	"\n"
	"A histogram is drawn with block characters, eight levels to a row; sca"
	"tter\n"
	"and line plots with braille dots, two by four to a character cell. The\n"
	"axes show the range of the data.\n";

static const char text_settings [] =
	"id: settings\n"
	"title: Settings\n"
	"summary: The settings file, the settings you can change and how.\n"
	"keywords: settings, config, set, mlcfg, ghost, highlight, theme, color"
	"s, preferences\n"
	"see: themes, keys, commands, cli\n"
	"\n"
	"All settings are kept in one plain text file, `settings.mlcfg`, in the\n"
	"settings folder: `%APPDATA%\\matlog` on Windows, `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mat"
	"log`\n"
	"or `~/.config/matlog` on Linux, and `~/Library/Application Support/mat"
	"log`\n"
	"on macOS. `MATLOG_CONFIG` names another folder. A missing file is a va"
	"lid\n"
	"configuration: every setting has a default.\n"
	"\n"
	"The file has `[sections]` and `key = value` lines, and `#` starts a co"
	"mment\n"
	"line. A line that does not make sense is ignored, and matlog keeps wha"
	"t it\n"
	"does not know when it writes the file:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"[display]\n"
	"theme = light\n"
	"ghost = off\n"
	"\n"
	"[keys]\n"
	"Ctrl+K = command\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# The settings\n"
	"```command\n"
	"theme      the colour theme: dark, light, contrast, mono or a name.the"
	"me file\n"
	"colors     24bit, 256, 16 or off; the default follows the terminal\n"
	"highlight  on or off: the syntax colours\n"
	"ghost      on or off: the dim preview of the result while you type\n"
	"watch      on or off: the watch window with the names and their values\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Changing them\n"
	"```command\n"
	":set               list the settings and their values\n"
	":set ghost         show one\n"
	":set ghost off     change it; the file is written\n"
	":config            this page\n"
	":config path       where the file is\n"
	":config reload     read the file again, the key bindings too\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"`matlog config` does the same without the screen: `matlog config list`"
	",\n"
	"`matlog config get ghost`, `matlog config set ghost off` and\n"
	"`matlog config path`. The key bindings have their own section, see\n"
	"[keys](keys); the folds of your sessions are kept in `[folds]`.\n";

static const char text_statistics [] =
	"id: statistics\n"
	"title: Statistics\n"
	"summary: Means, spread, correlation, line and polynomial fits, distrib"
	"utions.\n"
	"keywords: statistics, mean, median, mode, variance, std, standard devi"
	"ation, quantile, correlation, regression, linfit, polyfit, histogram, "
	"binomial, normal, poisson, zscore\n"
	"see: functions, matrices, ex-stats-describe-data\n"
	"\n"
	"Data is a list of numbers, written as a vector `[2, 4, 4, 5]` (a colum"
	"n) or\n"
	"a row `[[2, 4, 4, 5]]`, or made by `range(1, 10)`. A list has at most "
	"64\n"
	"numbers. The results are exact fractions while the data is exact.\n"
	"\n"
	"# One list\n"
	"```code\n"
	"sum count mean median mode       mode is the smallest of the most freq"
	"uent\n"
	"var std                          sample: divided by n - 1\n"
	"pvar pstd                        population: divided by n\n"
	"min max                          of a list too: min ([3, 1, 2])\n"
	"quantile (v, p)                  p from 0 to 1, linear between the val"
	"ues\n"
	"iqr (v)                          quantile 3/4 less quantile 1/4\n"
	"skew kurt                        skewness (approximate), excess kurtos"
	"is\n"
	"cumsum sort                      running sums, the data in order\n"
	"histogram (v, k)                 how many values are in each of k bins\n"
	"hist (v, k)                      the same as a plot, see plots\n"
	"range (a, b [, step])            a, a + step, ... up to b\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"mean([2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9])\n"
	"=> 5\n"
	"var([2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9])\n"
	"=> 32/7\n"
	"pstd([2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9])\n"
	"=> 2\n"
	"median([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n"
	"=> 3\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"A standard deviation is exact when the variance is a perfect square, e"
	"lse\n"
	"an approximation. Too little data is the error `domain` (the variance "
	"of one\n"
	"number), a spread of zero is `div_zero`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Two lists\n"
	"```code\n"
	"cov (x, y)        sample covariance\n"
	"corr (x, y)       the correlation coefficient\n"
	"rsq (x, y)        r squared, exact\n"
	"linfit (x, y)     [a, b] so that y is about a + b x\n"
	"polyfit (x, y, n) [c0, ..., cn] of the best polynomial of degree n\n"
	"lstsq (A, b)      the x with A x closest to b\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"linfit([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 4, 5, 9])\n"
	"=> [[-1/2], [11/5]]\n"
	"corr([1, 2, 3], [3, 2, 1])\n"
	"=> -1\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Counting and distributions\n"
	"```code\n"
	"binomial (n, k)  perm (n, k)      choose and arrange, exact\n"
	"binomial_pdf (n, k, p)            exact when p is exact\n"
	"poisson_pdf (k, rate)\n"
	"normal_pdf normal_cdf (x [, mu, sigma])\n"
	"normal_inv (p [, mu, sigma])      the x with normal_cdf (x) = p\n"
	"zscore (x, mu, sigma)             (x - mu)/sigma\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"binomial(10, 3)\n"
	"=> 120\n"
	"binomial_pdf(10, 3, 1/2)\n"
	"=> 15/128\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"The normal functions are approximations (the sign says so). See the ex"
	"amples\n"
	"[describing data](ex-stats-describe-data), [fitting a line](ex-stats-f"
	"it-a-line)\n"
	"and [coins and the bell curve](ex-stats-coin-and-bell-curve).\n";

static const char text_symbolic [] =
	"id: symbolic\n"
	"title: Symbolic math\n"
	"summary: Simplify, expand, differentiate, integrate, limits and equati"
	"ons.\n"
	"keywords: simplify, expand, subs, diff, derivative, integrate, integra"
	"l, limit, solve, equation, symbols\n"
	"see: syntax, commands\n"
	"\n"
	"Inside these functions names that have no value are symbols, so\n"
	"`diff(x^3, x)` works without defining `x`. Outside them, a name with n"
	"o\n"
	"value is an error unless symbols are turned on with `:symbols on` (see\n"
	"[commands](commands)); the examples on this page are written with symb"
	"ols\n"
	"on.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The functions\n"
	"- `simplify(f)` and `expand(f)` rewrite an expression.\n"
	"- `subs(f, x, v)` puts a value for a name.\n"
	"- `diff(f, x [, n])` the derivative, `n` times.\n"
	"- `integrate(f, x [, a, b])` an antiderivative, or the definite integr"
	"al\n"
	"  from `a` to `b`.\n"
	"- `limit(f, x, a [, side])` a limit; `inf` is infinity.\n"
	"- `solve(f, x)` the real roots of `f` as a set.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"simplify(x + x)\n"
	"=> 2x\n"
	"expand((x+1)^2)\n"
	"=> x\302\262 + 2x + 1\n"
	"diff(x^3, x)\n"
	"=> 3x\302\262\n"
	"integrate(x^2, x)\n"
	"=> x\302\263/3\n"
	"integrate(x^2, x, 0, 1)\n"
	"=> 1/3\n"
	"limit(sin(x)/x, x, 0)\n"
	"=> 1\n"
	"solve(x^2 - 4, x)\n"
	"=> {-2, 2}\n"
	"```\n";

static const char text_syntax [] =
	"id: syntax\n"
	"title: The algebra syntax\n"
	"summary: Numbers, operators, names and statements of a math line.\n"
	"keywords: precedence, operators, implicit multiplication, fractions, e"
	"xact, unicode, assignment\n"
	"see: functions, units, symbolic\n"
	"\n"
	"A math line in the algebra domain is an expression, an assignment\n"
	"(`a = 3`) or the definition of a function (`f(x) = x^2`). Results are "
	"exact\n"
	"whenever they can be: fractions stay fractions.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Numbers and operators\n"
	"Numbers are written `12`, `3.14` or `.5`. The operators are `+ - * / ^"
	"`\n"
	"and the postfix `!` for the factorial. `^` binds tighter than `*` and "
	"`/`,\n"
	"and is right associative; a minus in front binds looser than `^`.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"1/3 + 1/6\n"
	"=> 1/2\n"
	"-2^2\n"
	"=> -4\n"
	"2^3^2\n"
	"=> 512\n"
	"5!\n"
	"=> 120\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Implicit products\n"
	"A number, a closing parenthesis or a superscript directly followed by "
	"a\n"
	"name, an opening parenthesis or a root is a product: `2x`, `2(x+1)`,\n"
	"`(a)(b)`, `2pi`. Names are whole words, so `xy` is one name, not `x*y`"
	".\n"
	"\n"
	"# Unicode\n"
	"The unicode spellings are the same as the ASCII ones: `x\302\262` for "
	"`x^2`,\n"
	"`\342\210\232` for `sqrt`, `\317\200` for `pi`, `\303\227` and `"
	"\302\267` for `*`, `\303\267` for `/`, `\342\210\222` for `-`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Names and statements\n"
	"Names are letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit. A name\n"
	"that is assigned keeps its value in the lines below.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"a = 3\n"
	"=> 3\n"
	"2a + 1\n"
	"=> 7\n"
	"f(x) = x^2 + 1\n"
	"f(a)\n"
	"=> 10\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Exact and approximate results\n"
	"A result that cannot be an exact fraction is approximate and shown wit"
	"h\n"
	"`\342\211\210`: `sqrt(2)` is `\342\211\210 1.41421356`. The constants "
	"`pi` and `e` are exact\n"
	"names that evaluate to approximations.\n";

static const char text_themes [] =
	"id: themes\n"
	"title: Themes and settings\n"
	"summary: Colours, the built in themes, your own theme files and the se"
	"ttings file.\n"
	"keywords: theme, colors, colours, dark, light, contrast, mono, setting"
	"s, settings.mlcfg, background, highlight, no_color\n"
	"see: settings, commands\n"
	"\n"
	"Math lines are drawn with syntax highlighting: numbers, names, functio"
	"ns,\n"
	"constants, units, operators, brackets, keywords and errors each have a\n"
	"colour, and so have the gutter, the results and the hints.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Choosing a theme\n"
	"`:theme dark`, `:theme light`, `:theme contrast` or `:theme mono`; Tab\n"
	"completes the names, also those of your own theme files. `:colors 24bi"
	"t`,\n"
	"`256`, `16` or `off` sets how many colours the terminal is asked to sh"
	"ow;\n"
	"it is found from `COLORTERM`, `TERM` and `NO_COLOR` by default. The ch"
	"oice is\n"
	"kept in the settings file.\n"
	"\n"
	"The `light` and `contrast` themes paint their own background; `dark` a"
	"nd\n"
	"`mono` keep the colours of the terminal.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Your own theme\n"
	"A theme is a text file `name.theme` in the settings folder. Each line "
	"is\n"
	"`class = style`:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"background = #ffffff\n"
	"paint      = on\n"
	"number     = #098658\n"
	"error      = #cd3131 underline\n"
	"gutter     = #6e7781\n"
	"```\n"
	"A style is a colour `#rrggbb`, a background `on #rrggbb` and the words\n"
	"`bold`, `dim`, `underline`, `reverse`; `none` is no style. Classes tha"
	"t are\n"
	"left out keep the style of the dark theme. `matlog theme dark` prints "
	"a\n"
	"built in theme in this form to start from; `:theme reload` reads the f"
	"ile\n"
	"again, and it is read again by itself when you save it.\n"
	"\n"
	"The classes are text, number, name, function, constant, unit, operator"
	",\n"
	"paren, keyword, logic, binder, error, match, unmatched, gutter, result"
	", result-error,\n"
	"ghost, hint-key, note and note-code.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The settings file\n"
	"The theme and the colour level are two of the settings in `settings.ml"
	"cfg`;\n"
	"see [settings](settings) for the file, where it is and the other setti"
	"ngs.\n";

static const char text_tutorial [] =
	"id: tutorial\n"
	"title: The tutorial\n"
	"summary: Learn the tool by doing, inside the real editor, in small les"
	"sons.\n"
	"keywords: tutorial, lesson, learn, start, teach, beginner, practice\n"
	"see: editor, commands, keys\n"
	"\n"
	"The tutorial teaches matlog by doing. A panel at the top of the screen"
	" shows\n"
	"the lesson and the task; you do the task in the real editor, below it,"
	" and\n"
	"the panel moves on when it sees that you did. Nothing is simulated: th"
	"e\n"
	"tutorial only watches the keys and the session, so what you learn is e"
	"xactly\n"
	"what the tool does.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Starting and leaving\n"
	"```command\n"
	":tutorial          start, at the first lesson not finished yet\n"
	":tutorial syntax   start at the lessons about what to type\n"
	":tutorial next     skip the step (F6)\n"
	":tutorial back     go back a step (Shift+F6)\n"
	":tutorial show     type the model answer for the step\n"
	":tutorial reset    forget the finished lessons\n"
	":tutorial off      leave the tutorial; the session stays\n"
	"```\n"
	"`matlog --tutorial` starts it from the shell. A command that needs the\n"
	"session saved first asks; `:tutorial!` starts anyway.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The lessons\n"
	"The first stage is the tool: notes, math lines, moving, selecting and "
	"undo,\n"
	"domains and commands, results and steps, help and the watch window. Th"
	"e\n"
	"second stage is what to type: numbers and operators, names and functio"
	"ns,\n"
	"reading errors, matrices, derivatives and limits, units, complex numbe"
	"rs,\n"
	"quaternions and statistics, boolean algebra and lambda calculus.\n"
	"\n"
	"A wrong try is never an error. After a few keys the panel shows a hint"
	", and\n"
	"the finished lessons are remembered, so the next start continues after"
	" them.\n"
	"See [the editor](editor) and [the key bindings](keys).\n";

static const char text_units [] =
	"id: units\n"
	"title: Units\n"
	"summary: Quantities with units, conversions, SI prefixes and plural na"
	"mes.\n"
	"keywords: unit, quantity, conversion, to, meter, second, hour, day, we"
	"ek, month, year, ampere hour, prefix\n"
	"see: syntax\n"
	"\n"
	"A number followed by a unit is a quantity. Quantities add and multiply\n"
	"with their dimensions checked, and `a to b` converts.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"5 km + 300 m\n"
	"=> 5300 m\n"
	"3 ft to m\n"
	"=> 1143/1250 m\n"
	"2 hours to min\n"
	"=> 120 min\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Names\n"
	"Units have a symbol (`km`) and a long name (`kilometer`); the SI prefi"
	"xes\n"
	"work on both, and a long name may be plural (`3 days`). Other time uni"
	"ts are\n"
	"the `week`, the `month` (a twelfth of a year) and the `year` of 365 da"
	"ys.\n"
	"Charge can be written in ampere hours: `5 mAh to C` is `18 C`.\n"
	"\n"
	"A unit name that is also a variable means the variable: names are look"
	"ed up\n"
	"as variables first.\n"
	"\n"
	"`dim(x)` gives the dimension of a quantity as one SI unit.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"1 year to days\n"
	"=> 365 days\n"
	"5 mAh to C\n"
	"=> 18 C\n"
	"```\n";

